# `bench run` writes two kinds of artifact under .bench/, and they are
# deliberately treated differently.
#
# .bench/runs/<tag>/ — per-run report.md + report.json. Transient, large,
# regenerable from the same --seed/--tag, and named after a timestamp by
# default, so tracking them would add an unbounded stream of one-off files.
# Ignored. (This repo has a documented `git add -A` hazard; an explicit rule
# is what keeps a stray sweep out of a commit.)
/.bench/runs/

# .bench/history.jsonl — NOT ignored, on purpose. The spec frames it as the
# cross-run trend ledger: one append-only line per run, tying a git SHA +
# seed + model set to the scores it produced. Its whole value is being
# comparable across time and across machines, which requires it to be in
# version control. Small, append-only, and merge-friendly (conflicts resolve
# by keeping both lines). If you are tempted to ignore it, you are throwing
# away the only record of how this benchmark has moved.
